{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-168600" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An object generates an electric field given by $F(x,y)=(a{y}^{3}-1)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j$ in a planar coordinate system, where $a$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCurrent part:\n\nGiven that $F(x,y)$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/state.json" "file_sha256" "c639292e0528d0cad5a639b9995d14b52125d3e3a9b73a8358fcd6db334e4822"} {"question" "q-168600" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n>$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor our vector field $F,$ the domain is $D={R}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP=a{y}^{3}-1,\\,Q=3x{y}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3a{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a{y}^{2} & =3{y}^{2} \\\\ 3a & =3 \\\\ a & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/state.json" "file_sha256" "c639292e0528d0cad5a639b9995d14b52125d3e3a9b73a8358fcd6db334e4822"} {"question" "q-168600" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "85bfb03a56a0d8f686d7e0399b627554f8f7a799e688f535788a359730d105ff"} {"question" "q-168600" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/state.json" "file_sha256" "c639292e0528d0cad5a639b9995d14b52125d3e3a9b73a8358fcd6db334e4822"} {"question" "q-168600" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/state.json" "file_sha256" "c639292e0528d0cad5a639b9995d14b52125d3e3a9b73a8358fcd6db334e4822"} {"question" "q-168601" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An object generates an electric field given by $F(x,y)=(a{y}^{3}-1)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j$ in a planar coordinate system, where $a$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nGiven that $F(x,y)$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n$\\text{div}\\,F=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/state.json" "file_sha256" "c639292e0528d0cad5a639b9995d14b52125d3e3a9b73a8358fcd6db334e4822"} {"question" "q-168601" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn part 1, we found that $a=1.$ So, \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=({y}^{3}-1)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{3}-1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x{y}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =0+6xy \\\\  & =6xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/state.json" "file_sha256" "c639292e0528d0cad5a639b9995d14b52125d3e3a9b73a8358fcd6db334e4822"} {"question" "q-168601" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "85bfb03a56a0d8f686d7e0399b627554f8f7a799e688f535788a359730d105ff"} {"question" "q-168601" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/state.json" "file_sha256" "c639292e0528d0cad5a639b9995d14b52125d3e3a9b73a8358fcd6db334e4822"} {"question" "q-168601" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6xy"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/state.json" "file_sha256" "c639292e0528d0cad5a639b9995d14b52125d3e3a9b73a8358fcd6db334e4822"} {"question" "q-168602" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An object generates an electric field given by $F(x,y)=(a{y}^{3}-1)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j$ in a planar coordinate system, where $a$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nGiven that $F(x,y)$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n$\\text{div}\\,F=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $6xy$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x,y)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $+\\,C,$\nwhere $C$ is a constant-" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/state.json" "file_sha256" "c639292e0528d0cad5a639b9995d14b52125d3e3a9b73a8358fcd6db334e4822"} {"question" "q-168602" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In part 1, we found that $a=1.$ So, \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=({y}^{3}-1)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j.\n$$\n\nThe gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={y}^{3}-1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3x{y}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int ({y}^{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{y}^{3}-x+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3x{y}^{2}+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3x{y}^{2}+1$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x{y}^{2}+{g}^{′}(y) & =3x{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =1 \\\\ g(y) & =y+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=y+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x{y}^{3}-x+y+C.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/state.json" "file_sha256" "c639292e0528d0cad5a639b9995d14b52125d3e3a9b73a8358fcd6db334e4822"} {"question" "q-168602" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "85bfb03a56a0d8f686d7e0399b627554f8f7a799e688f535788a359730d105ff"} {"question" "q-168602" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/state.json" "file_sha256" "c639292e0528d0cad5a639b9995d14b52125d3e3a9b73a8358fcd6db334e4822"} {"question" "q-168602" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "xy^3-x+y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/state.json" "file_sha256" "c639292e0528d0cad5a639b9995d14b52125d3e3a9b73a8358fcd6db334e4822"} {"question" "q-168603" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An object generates an electric field given by $F(x,y)=(a{y}^{3}-1)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j$ in a planar coordinate system, where $a$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nGiven that $F(x,y)$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n$\\text{div}\\,F=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $6xy$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nFind $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x,y)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $xy^3-x+y$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $+\\,C,$\nwhere $C$ is a constant-\n\nCurrent part:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/assets/642a546fc498dfb0f01c0729959f9f68b8dc2d142d966673f204eda910f19211.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            Consider the square $C$ with side length $8$ centered at $O,$ shown above. The flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{1}$ is given by\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}^{}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}s.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/state.json" "file_sha256" "c639292e0528d0cad5a639b9995d14b52125d3e3a9b73a8358fcd6db334e4822"} {"question" "q-168603" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{1}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $x$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $n=i.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/assets/1939a274191b89f69e51a929654489ed9038df0313f6e47b15e0ff55ad813066.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(({y}^{3}-1)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}{y}^{3}-1\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/state.json" "file_sha256" "c639292e0528d0cad5a639b9995d14b52125d3e3a9b73a8358fcd6db334e4822"} {"question" "q-168603" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "85bfb03a56a0d8f686d7e0399b627554f8f7a799e688f535788a359730d105ff"} {"question" "q-168603" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/state.json" "file_sha256" "c639292e0528d0cad5a639b9995d14b52125d3e3a9b73a8358fcd6db334e4822"} {"question" "q-168603" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y^3-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/state.json" "file_sha256" "c639292e0528d0cad5a639b9995d14b52125d3e3a9b73a8358fcd6db334e4822"} {"question" "q-168604" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An object generates an electric field given by $F(x,y)=(a{y}^{3}-1)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j$ in a planar coordinate system, where $a$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nGiven that $F(x,y)$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n$\\text{div}\\,F=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $6xy$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nFind $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x,y)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $xy^3-x+y$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $+\\,C,$\nwhere $C$ is a constant-\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/assets/642a546fc498dfb0f01c0729959f9f68b8dc2d142d966673f204eda910f19211.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            Consider the square $C$ with side length $8$ centered at $O,$ shown above. The flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{1}$ is given by\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}^{}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $y^3-1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}s.$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind the average value of the function $f(x,y)=x{y}^{3}-x+y$ inside the square defined in part 4.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}_{\\text{ave}}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/state.json" "file_sha256" "c639292e0528d0cad5a639b9995d14b52125d3e3a9b73a8358fcd6db334e4822"} {"question" "q-168604" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The interior of the square defined in the previous part can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y)\\,:\\,-4<x<4,\\,-4<y<4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(R)=8\\cdot 8=64.\n$$\n\nApplying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(R)}\\,{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{64}{∬}_{R}(x{y}^{3}-x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{64}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}(x{y}^{3}-x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{64}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}{[\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{4}-xy+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{y=-4}^{y=4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{64}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}[(64x-4x+8)-(64x+4x+8)]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{64}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}(-8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{-4}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}(16-16) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/state.json" "file_sha256" "c639292e0528d0cad5a639b9995d14b52125d3e3a9b73a8358fcd6db334e4822"} {"question" "q-168604" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "85bfb03a56a0d8f686d7e0399b627554f8f7a799e688f535788a359730d105ff"} {"question" "q-168604" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/state.json" "file_sha256" "c639292e0528d0cad5a639b9995d14b52125d3e3a9b73a8358fcd6db334e4822"} {"question" "q-168604" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/state.json" "file_sha256" "c639292e0528d0cad5a639b9995d14b52125d3e3a9b73a8358fcd6db334e4822"} {"question" "q-168605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An object generates an electric field given by $F(x,y)=(a{y}^{3}-1)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j$ in a planar coordinate system, where $a$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nGiven that $F(x,y)$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n$\\text{div}\\,F=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $6xy$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nFind $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x,y)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $xy^3-x+y$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $+\\,C,$\nwhere $C$ is a constant-\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/assets/642a546fc498dfb0f01c0729959f9f68b8dc2d142d966673f204eda910f19211.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            Consider the square $C$ with side length $8$ centered at $O,$ shown above. The flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{1}$ is given by\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}^{}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $y^3-1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}s.$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nFind the average value of the function $f(x,y)=x{y}^{3}-x+y$ inside the square defined in part 4.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}_{\\text{ave}}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $0$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/assets/6fd34e9070cd9555ba790e7300bb78b4d19f1b71b231e8a92f4e8f306c3d4623.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The region $D$ in the first quadrant is enclosed between two quarter-circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $3,$ as shown above. \nIn polar coordinates, the following integral can be written as\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/state.json" "file_sha256" "c639292e0528d0cad5a639b9995d14b52125d3e3a9b73a8358fcd6db334e4822"} {"question" "q-168605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the disk enclosed between the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/state.json" "file_sha256" "c639292e0528d0cad5a639b9995d14b52125d3e3a9b73a8358fcd6db334e4822"} {"question" "q-168605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "85bfb03a56a0d8f686d7e0399b627554f8f7a799e688f535788a359730d105ff"} {"question" "q-168605" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/state.json" "file_sha256" "c639292e0528d0cad5a639b9995d14b52125d3e3a9b73a8358fcd6db334e4822"} {"question" "q-168605" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "r^2\\cos(\\theta)+r^2\\sin(\\theta)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/state.json" "file_sha256" "c639292e0528d0cad5a639b9995d14b52125d3e3a9b73a8358fcd6db334e4822"} {"question" "q-168606" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An object generates an electric field given by $F(x,y)=(a{y}^{3}-1)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j$ in a planar coordinate system, where $a$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nGiven that $F(x,y)$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n$\\text{div}\\,F=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $6xy$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nFind $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x,y)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $xy^3-x+y$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $+\\,C,$\nwhere $C$ is a constant-\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/assets/642a546fc498dfb0f01c0729959f9f68b8dc2d142d966673f204eda910f19211.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            Consider the square $C$ with side length $8$ centered at $O,$ shown above. The flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{1}$ is given by\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}^{}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $y^3-1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}s.$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nFind the average value of the function $f(x,y)=x{y}^{3}-x+y$ inside the square defined in part 4.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}_{\\text{ave}}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $0$\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/assets/6fd34e9070cd9555ba790e7300bb78b4d19f1b71b231e8a92f4e8f306c3d4623.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The region $D$ in the first quadrant is enclosed between two quarter-circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $3,$ as shown above. \nIn polar coordinates, the following integral can be written as\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $r^2\\cos(\\theta)+r^2\\sin(\\theta)$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind the total charge $Q$ of a thin metal plate that occupies the region $D$ defined in part 6 and has charge density $\\rho (x,y)=x+y.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $Q=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/state.json" "file_sha256" "c639292e0528d0cad5a639b9995d14b52125d3e3a9b73a8358fcd6db334e4822"} {"question" "q-168606" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the region $D$ represents a quarter-annulus enclosed between the circles of the radii $1$ and $3$ centered at the origin.\n\nThe total charge $Q$ of an object occupying the region $D$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=x+y$ is given by\n\n$$\nQ={∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn part 6, by transforming to polar coordinates, we found that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{r}^{3}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0))\\cdot (9-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{26}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{52}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the total charge of the object is $Q=\\frac{52}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/state.json" "file_sha256" "c639292e0528d0cad5a639b9995d14b52125d3e3a9b73a8358fcd6db334e4822"} {"question" "q-168606" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "85bfb03a56a0d8f686d7e0399b627554f8f7a799e688f535788a359730d105ff"} {"question" "q-168606" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/state.json" "file_sha256" "c639292e0528d0cad5a639b9995d14b52125d3e3a9b73a8358fcd6db334e4822"} {"question" "q-168606" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{52}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/state.json" "file_sha256" "c639292e0528d0cad5a639b9995d14b52125d3e3a9b73a8358fcd6db334e4822"}] "usage" {} "basis" 2845 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
